Frequently Asked Questions
How can I get data from non-Splunk Search sources?

You can create a custom datasource to access data from virtually any resource. The only requirement is the data sent to the search observer is returned in the form of a DataSet and a properly formatted meta object. You can find more detailed instructions on creating Custom DataSources in the Advanced Section. Backend services are responsible for enforcing CORS to enable cross-server communication.

Example

The following is a simple REST-based datasource which fetches weather data for Chicago, IL from the National Weather Service.

Note: For simplicity, this example does not contain any error handling, or use data passed via options or context, for configuration and authentication respectively.

import { DataSource } from '@splunk/datasources';
import { DataSet } from '@splunk/datasource-utils';
import fetch from 'node-fetch';

export default class WeatherDatasource extends DataSource {
    // Execute the search during setup (fetch the weather, save a promise)
    setup() {
        this.api = fetch(
            'https://api.weather.gov/gridpoints/TOP/31,80/forecast'
        );
    }

    // Execute the status/results observable flow
    request() {
        return (observable) => {
            this.api
                // Process the response into JSON
                .then((res) => {
                    return res.json();
                })
                .then((res) => {
                    // Create a DataSet
                    const data = DataSet.fromJSONArray(
                        [
                            { name: 'name' },
                            { name: 'startTime' },
                            { name: 'endTime' },
                            { name: 'temperature' },
                            { name: 'windSpeed' },
                            { name: 'windDirection' },
                            { name: 'shortForecast' },
                        ],
                        res.properties.periods
                    );
                    // Populate metaData
                    const meta = {
                        status: 'done',
                        totalCount: res.properties.periods.length,
                    };

                    // Push data to the observer
                    observable.next({ data, meta });
                    // Inform the observer will be no more data
                    observable.complete();
                });

            // Do nothing in observable teardown
            return () => {};
        };
    }
}
What packages are provided by the UDF team, and what do they contain?

The UDF team supplies numerous packages in the dashboard ecosystem. While most of these are pulled in automatically based on their usage within UDF components, some of them provide base implementations that should be extended when customizing a UDF app.

Package

Purpose


@splunk/dashboard

Provides the editing interface for dashboards


@splunk/dashboard-action-buttons

Visualization menu buttons for different actions


@splunk/dashboard-context

React Context to hold APIs and metadata used throughout the library


@splunk/dashboard-core

Renders the dashboard canvas and its contents


@splunk/dashboard-datasource-panel

Provides a DataSource list for the Data Overview panel


@splunk/dashboard-definition

Utilities for definition, modification, and validation


@splunk/dashboard-editors

Bespoke editors for search, layouts, and drilldowns


@splunk/dashboard-event-handlers

Implements automated responses to visualization interaction events


@splunk/dashboard-icons

A set of custom icons used throughout the UDF user interface


@splunk/dashboard-inputs

Custom inputs for dashboards


@splunk/dashboard-layouts

Implementations for visualization, placement, and canvas interactions


@splunk/dashboard-presets

Mappings for Viz, Layout, Input, etc to their implementations


@splunk/dashboard-search

Manages search infrastructure


@splunk/dashboard-state

Manages global redux layer


@splunk/dashboard-telemetry

Component for talking to telemetry providers, esp. Splunk Web Analytics


@splunk/dashboard-toolbar

Components related to the dashboard toolbar


@splunk/dashboard-types

Shared typescript types for development


@splunk/dashboard-ui

Shared React components used throughout UDF


@splunk/dashboard-utils

Shared utility methods used throughout UDF


@splunk/dashboard-validation

Utilities for validation of a dashboard definition


@splunk/datasource-utils

Interface for working with DataSources


@splunk/datasources

Implementations for communicating with Splunk Search services

